A||N||27||Toogy||Getting rid of the GAP||||4||
Z||000000||Toogy||11-29-2001||07:04 AM||||Ok, the GT that I am about to get has a huge fenderwell gap.  You now what I mean.  What is the best way to get rid of it?  It currently has 215/50R15 on the front and 225/50R15 on the back.  I can't afford new rims.  SO I was thinking 1" Lower springs and maybe a 60 series tire or something?<BR>Will this help at all or do I really need bigger rims.  I am just looking for an easy solution to get rid of most of the gap.<BR>P.S. what are the widest tires you can fit on the back of a fiero?  Will 245/60R15 fit on the back?  What about the front?<BR>Sorry for all the questions.<P>Thanks<P>------------------<BR><B>Soon to be: 1987 GT 5-speed</B>||||reg||4
Z||000001||isthiswhereiputausername?||11-29-2001||07:34 AM||||instead of paying the money out for new tires or rims,  why not just lower it?  you can get lowering springs for the front & rear and they would look great on it.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||hugh||11-29-2001||07:49 AM||||Fill the passenger side with pineapples,the weight will lower it.||||reg||12
Z||000003||Toogy||11-29-2001||08:21 AM||||How much can I lower it before I start to have to worry about how and where I drive.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Formula88||11-29-2001||11:20 AM||||If you have the factory 15x7" rims on the back, you can run a 245/50-15 and the tire will be only slightly shorter than the stock 215/60-15.  Or you can run a 225/60-15 if you want something slightly taller.  A lot of people here recommend against the 245's on a 7" wide rim, but that's the recommended rim width for the Dunlop SP8000 in that size.  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||FieroBUZZ||11-29-2001||11:32 AM||||I'm in the same situation.  My SE project has 16" rims but still a nice gap.  The roads here are such that I'm not sure I should lower it, though.<P>QUESTION:  I always believed that if you used shorter springs you should use shorter shocks as well. Not true????<P>Gary <A HREF="http://www.grinnin.0catch.com" TARGET=_blank>www.grinnin.0catch.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Toogy||11-29-2001||11:45 AM||||They are not the factory 15x7's.  I have no idea what width they are.  how do I measure?  <P>What are some good tires in the 60 series range?||||reg||1
Z||000007||terryk||11-29-2001||02:46 PM||||With ST or Eibach's you don't need shorter shocks or struts. Both springs lower the car 1.5 to 2" plus. I say plus become I've got Eibach's on my 86SE and I've installed ST and Eibach's in other Fiero's and I swear they dropped more than 2". Anything more than that is a problem for camber adjustment anyway. Bang for the buck, either are good.||||reg||1
Z||000008||stingant||11-29-2001||04:50 PM||||I have BF Goodrich t/a tires on my back. They are 225/60 H rated. I autocross and use these, they are awsome for the price..at Sams Club I paid $60 a piece..you can't beat that! The best brand I have heard is the yokahoma tires.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Alex4mula||11-29-2001||05:19 PM||||New rims will do nothing unless you change the tire diameter. Best way is to lower (cut/buy springs) the car and to use 60 series tires. Both 215/50 and 225/50 has smaller diameter than stock 205-215/60 stock tires.<P>------------------<BR>    Alex4mula<BR>:) Red-Original Owner & White-1/94 with PM (:||||reg||1
Z||000010||ray b||11-29-2001||05:46 PM||||cheapest way is a fat g/f[old lowrider j/k]<BR>bigger tires may f-up your speedo rate<BR>if you go tooo far from stock size dia.<BR>fender flares ??? anybody try that????<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||6
Z||000011||Smoooooth GT||11-29-2001||11:54 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by hugh:<BR><B>Fill the passenger side with pineapples,the weight will lower it.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>lmao....  would shrink it like this??<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/vroom_5.gif"> <P>------------------<BR>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/smooooothgtsig_9.gif">              <BR>      <P>www.thefibersmith.com<BR>Technical  Support (903) 825-7744 Steve<BR>Shaved door handles, sidetrim, exhaust outlet's, etc.... smooooth!!!||||reg||1
Z||000012||Haze_Performance||11-30-2001||12:17 AM||||I'm running Eibach lowering springs with 225/50/16's on 7" wheels... If you drive the car a lot... Go with rear coilovers & drop spindles on the front.. Your ride will be much softer. I drive my '85 just about everyday.. I'm working on pulling the lowering springs out & replace it with the other setup..<P><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/MyCar0004.jpg"> <BR>Lowering spring alone are a kidney buster over the potholes...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/goodlife.gif">    <BR><FONT SIZE="1"><FONT COLOR="purple">Goto Website - <A HREF="http://www.ClassicFiero.com" TARGET=_blank>Click Here</A> </FONT></FONT>||||reg||14
Z||000013||rogergarrison||11-30-2001||07:02 AM||||wider tires wont do a thing for the gap. you could have tires 3 feet wide and the gap is still there. All you can do is put on 'taller' tires and wheels, which will throw your speedo off and change your gear ratio, OR lower the car with lowering springs. I put on taller tires myself, i had an aftermarket speedo anyway and it gave me more top end speed, less power off the line and a little better gas milage<P>------------------<BR>           <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/rogergarrison1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||Alex4mula||11-30-2001||11:23 AM||||Wider tires will be taller if same aspect ratio is kept. Sidewall height is a percentage of tire width. Just math.<P>------------------<BR>    Alex4mula<BR>:) Red-Original Owner & White-1/94 with PM (:||||reg||1
Z||000015||rogergarrison||11-30-2001||12:34 PM||||maybe not. my car has 225/60/16 and its a full inch taller diameter than my vette with 255/50/16. I know cause I just measured both with a tape. maybe if you put 335/50 theyd be same  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000016||Mr.Frosty||11-30-2001||02:47 PM||||But if you do the math,technically 225/60 is 135mm and 255/50 is 127.5mm of treadwall height.Some manufacturers designs tend to have taller(or deeper)tread.The measurements only tell you sidewall height,not tread depth.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT Turbo intercooled||||reg||1
Z||000017||Mr.Frosty||11-30-2001||02:51 PM||||I almost forgot.....Lower it please."84-87" Drop spindles up front,lowering springs in back."88" lowering springs.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT Turbo intercooled||||reg||1
Z||000018||Toogy||11-30-2001||03:10 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mr.Frosty:<BR><B>I almost forgot.....Lower it please."84-87" Drop spindles up front,lowering springs in back."88" lowering springs.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Where do I get this stuff from?||||reg||1
Z||000019||Mr.Frosty||11-30-2001||04:22 PM||||I got my drop spindles from Belltech.I can't find their website;I don't know if they even have one.You'll have to go through another company to get them.That's what my Dad and I had to do.They specialize in truck spindles,but they carry Fiero too.Expect $225.00 though.Your ride will stay the same,but your gauranteed to drop 2 inches in the wheel wells.The springs can be had from The fiero store. <A HREF="http://www.fierostore.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fierostore.com</A> <BR>I hope this helps,I'll try to find the reciept for them.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT Turbo intercooled||||reg||1
Z||000020||Toogy||11-30-2001||04:26 PM||||Excuse my ignoracne again, but what are drop spindles?<BR>On fierostore.com all I see is a complete set of eiback lowering springs.  How do I just get the rears?<P>Sorry, I am totally new to this kind of stuff.  I also have a Jeep and the whole concept of lowering vehicles is completely new to me.||||reg||1
Z||000021||Htown86GT||11-30-2001||04:28 PM||||My Sig - 17's - Eibach Sport 1 1/2 drop<P>------------------<BR>86' GT  17inch Cobras Sus-tec lowered<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://home.swbell.net/ravi1/myfiero.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000022||Mr.Frosty||11-30-2001||04:34 PM||||I found a place that specializes in Fiero's go here <A HREF="http://www.iserv.net/~strdream/dropspindles.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.iserv.net/~strdream/dropspindles.html</A> <BR>  As for the springs the Fiero store sells them in pairs too,just keep looking.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT Turbo intercooled||||reg||1
Z||000023||Mr.Frosty||11-30-2001||04:39 PM||||Dop spindles let you significantly lower the car,and the center of gravity without changing suspension geometry.Improving handling and keeping a soft ride.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT Turbo intercooled||||reg||1
Z||000024||rogergarrison||11-30-2001||04:44 PM||||dropped spindles move the center of front axle closer to the top control arm and farther from the lower arm. In effect it moves the center of the wheel assembly farther 'up' into the wheel opening making the body sit lower to the ground and preserving the factory alignment geometry. Lowering springs are specially made to have the same or similar suspension characteristics with a shorter spring height which also lowers the car body to the ground. Ideally, drop and rear coils are best, coil-overs are a good substitute. They combine the spring and shock into one compact unit that can be set at varying heights to suit you. Next best is all four lowering springs. Last you can cut the spring to lower it, but spring may not sit in its socket properly and you do change the spring rate, usually making the ride rougher and easier to bottom out on bumps.<P>------------------<BR>            <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/rogergarrison1.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 11-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000025||Toogy||11-30-2001||04:49 PM||||Thanks guys.  Like I said this is all new to me.  How hard is it to work on Fiero suspensions?  I know that Jeep's are super easy.||||reg||1
Z||000026||Mr.Frosty||11-30-2001||05:07 PM||||It all depends on how much time it spent getting to know Mother Nature.If it was garaged,which it looks by the pictures I saw,then pretty easy.If it was driven year round with little done,your gonna need an impact wrench,a hammer,and lots of penetrating lube.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>85 GT Turbo intercooled||||reg||1
Z||000027||hugh||11-30-2001||05:41 PM||||Smooth,No it won't shrink,It will smell real good---------------for a while.||||reg||7
